    {
      "filename": "Martinique - St. Pierre - The Prison (51358866282).jpg",
      "title": "File:Martinique - St. Pierre - The Prison (51358866282).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Martinique_-_St._Pierre_-_The_Prison_%2851358866282%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martinique_-_St._Pierre_-_The_Prison_(51358866282).jpg",
      "width": 4896,
      "height": 3672,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "Larry Syverson from Richmond, VA, USA",
      "description": "At 8:00 am on the morning of May 8, 1902, Mt. Pelee erupted sending a pyroclastic cloud of hot gases and ash on the city of Saint Pierre on the Caribbean island of Martinique.  The city was destroyed and over 30,000 people were killed instantly.\nThese are the ruins of the prison. \nAt 8:00 am on the morning of May 8, 1902, Mt. Pelee erupted sending a pyroclastic cloud of hot gases and ash on the city of Saint Pierre on the Caribbean island of Martinique.  The city was destroyed and over 30,000 people were killed instantly.\nThese are the ruins of the prison. \nIn the distance, you can see the opening to Sylbaris' crypt. \n\nOnly one person survived the destruction of the city:  Ludger Sylbaris.  He was a prisoner in this crypt in the dungeon of the jail.\n\nBadly burned, he was rescued four days after the eruption when a rescue team heard his cries.\n\nThe crypt (actually a munitions magazine) was built about 1660.",
      "dateCreated": "2020-01-18 11:30",
